para: '<p>Tequila and vodka are diverging sharply. Tequila R12 9L growth is <span class="qa-cite" data-measure="volume_growth" data-dims=\'{"category":"tequila"}\' data-value="+11.2%">+11.2%</span> versus vodka at <span class="qa-cite" data-measure="volume_growth" data-dims=\'{"category":"vodka"}\' data-value="-1.4%">-1.4%</span> — a 12.6-point spread that is the widest gap between any two major spirits categories in the dataset.</p><p>The story is sub-channel specific. Tequila is strongest in <span class="qa-cite" data-measure="volume_growth" data-dims=\'{"category":"tequila","subch":"Casual Dining"}\' data-value="+14.8%">casual dining (+14.8%)</span> and <span class="qa-cite" data-measure="volume_growth" data-dims=\'{"category":"tequila","subch":"Full-Bar"}\' data-value="+9.3%">full-bar (+9.3%)</span>, where premium agave consumption concentrates. Vodka is bleeding most in <span class="qa-cite" data-measure="volume_growth" data-dims=\'{"category":"vodka","subch":"Liquor — Chain"}\' data-value="-3.1%">chain accounts (-3.1%)</span>, where the standard tier dominates and consumer migration to other categories is most visible.</p>',
